# {{FACTORY_NAME}}

A [jigs](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs) factory: the pipelines this
machine runs, the bindings they run against, and the service that hosts them.

## Layout

- `jigs.config.ts` — the pipelines this factory hosts, keyed by the name
  `jigs run` takes. `jigs build` generates the service entry from it.
- `pipelines/` — the pipeline bodies, and beside them `review-loop.ts`, this
  factory's own composition of the review loop over the blocks jigs ships.
  Edit either freely. A file's path is half of the workflow id its
  `"use workflow"` body owns, so renaming or moving a pipeline changes that
  id; `review-loop.ts` carries no directive and owns no id at all.
  `review-loop.test.ts` beside it holds that composition to its sequence —
  the order, the CI bound, the merge policy and the endings — with the step
  wrappers faked and the blocks themselves running for real.
- `steps/jigs.ts` — this factory's `"use step"` wrappers around the steps jigs
  ships, and the blocks wired on top of them. Every step a pipeline calls
  comes from here, and each step id is this path plus the function's name.
  `jigs init` wrote it once and never rewrites it; a step jigs grows later
  gets its wrapper added by hand.
- `steps/describe-pr.ts` — the title and body a pull request opens with. jigs
  owns the mechanics; the conventions here, and the repair applied when an
  answer drifts out of them, are this factory's voice and the part to rewrite.
- `jigs.config.test.ts` — what the factory declares, and the ids the last
  build emitted.
- `jigs.yml` — this factory's service and dashboard ports and its bindings.
- `docker-compose.yml` — this factory's own Postgres World.

## Setup

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # then fill in LINEAR_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN
pnpm install              # once: this factory's own jigs, in node_modules/.bin
pnpm exec jigs up         # World, bootstrap, build, start, doctor
pnpm exec jigs bind git@github.com:owner/repo.git
pnpm exec jigs service restart   # the service clones every binding when it starts
```

Every `jigs` below is this factory's own: `pnpm exec jigs …`.

The service listens on `:{{SERVICE_PORT}}`, its dashboard on
`:{{DASHBOARD_PORT}}`, its World on `:{{POSTGRES_PORT}}`.

Renaming or moving an exported wrapper in `steps/jigs.ts`, or a pipeline file
under `pipelines/`, changes a durable id: do it only when `jigs ps` shows no
parked runs. A run whose id moved under it shows up stalled — cancel it and
relaunch.

## Pipelines

- `ship` — a ticket to a merged pull request: claim the ticket, snapshot it,
  review it into a brief, cut a worktree, then the review loop (implement ⇄
  review → PR → gate → merge → teardown). Takes the ticket as `ABC-123` or its
  uuid, `binding` names the repo, and `merge` is `jigs` (squash-merge on
  approval) or `human` (a person presses merge).

## Running a pipeline

```sh
jigs run ship --input ticket=ABC-123 --input binding=repo --input merge=human
jigs ps
jigs logs <run>
jigs cancel <run>
```

`<run>` is a run id, a unique prefix of one, or the ticket the run claimed.
Runs outlive the terminal that launched them: the service owns them.

Every launch preflights first, so an empty `.env` refuses the run rather than
creating one that fails later. `jigs doctor` runs the same checks without
launching anything.

## Development

```sh
pnpm typecheck
jigs build
pnpm test                 # jigs.config.ts's assertions, and the built ids
```

`pnpm test` reads the workflow and step ids out of the last build's
`.output/server/index.mjs`, so build first.

## Adding a pipeline

New file in `pipelines/`, exporting a `<name>Inputs` zod schema and a
`<name>Pipeline` function whose body opens with `"use workflow"`. Register it
in `jigs.config.ts` under the name `jigs run` should take, then `jigs build`
and `pnpm test`.

A pipeline imports its steps from `../steps/jigs.ts`, never from a
`@salimhamed/jigs` subpath: a step taken from the package would be addressed by
the package's version, and every jigs upgrade would rename it. Steps alone —
`pipelines/ship.ts` imports `ticketInput`, `claimTicket`, `claude` and `codex`
from the package directly, because none of those carries an id. A pipeline may
also declare `"use step"` functions in its own file; their ids come out as
`step//./pipelines/<file>//<fn>`, so the same rename rule applies to those
names.

`agent`, `ask`, `needsHuman`, `gate`, `agentOrHalt` and `ticketReview` are
compositions over those steps and carry no id; `steps/jigs.ts` exports them
already wired. The review loop is one more composition, except that this
factory writes it: `pipelines/review-loop.ts` calls the blocks jigs ships —
`implementAndReview`, `answerAsBuilder`, `fixCi`, `commitLeftoverWork`,
`postAnswers`, `describePr` — in the order and with the policy it chooses.

## Upgrading jigs

```sh
jigs upgrade              # or: jigs upgrade --to <version>
```

`upgrade` bumps `@salimhamed/jigs`, runs `jigs up`, then `pnpm typecheck`.
Installing needs a token that can read GitHub Packages in `~/.npmrc`; the
`.npmrc` here only routes the scope.

A release that grows a step needs a wrapper in `steps/jigs.ts`; the typecheck
names the gap. A release that moves one of the four runtime packages this
factory supplies — `@workflow/web`, `@workflow/world-postgres`, `workflow`,
`zod` — fails the install by name; move the same pin in `package.json` and run
`jigs upgrade` again.
